  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The <b>Configure a new CloudFront Distribution...</b> option is the best and simplest solution for a web application with high read traffic spikes. <b>CloudFront</b> is AWS's global content delivery network (CDN), designed to cache and distribute content efficiently. Directing Route53 to CloudFront minimizes direct loads on your ELB and EC2 fleet by serving cached content to users, significantly reducing traffic to your backend and cutting operational costs during traffic surges.<br><br>Here's why the others aren't optimal:<br><br><i>Deploy a Memcached cluster with ElastiCache...</i>—while ElastiCache is useful for dynamic, data-driven apps, it requires application changes to cache responses, and is not as universally effective at reducing network/compute costs as CloudFront for general content delivery.<br><br><i>Establish a fresh ELB with an added Auto Scaling Group tier...</i>—adding another ELB and EC2 tier adds complexity and cost instead of removing it. This doesn't address the root read-scaling problem and can lead to overprovisioning.<br><br><i>Asynchronously store commonly accessed responses in S3 buckets...</i>—redirecting to S3 for responses is possible, but not as seamless, integrated, or low-latency for dynamic content compared to CloudFront, and would require significant changes to your app logic.<br><br><b>Tip:</b> For read-heavy workloads, a CDN like CloudFront is usually the first recommendation for scaling delivery efficiently and reducing backend costs.</p>",
  "query": "<p>Your web application is experiencing high expenses due to sudden increases in read-heavy traffic (90% reads, 10% writes), which are especially frequent during newsworthy events. Currently, all requests are routed via a Route53 Alias Record targeting an AWS ELB, which manages an Auto Scaling Group of EC2 instances. What is the most straightforward and cost-effective method to handle and scale for these bursts in read queries?</p>",
